\section{Unified ggplot2 return contract}{

All \code{plot()} S3 methods now render with \code{ggplot2} (\verb{ggplot2 (>= 3.4.0)} in
\code{Imports}) and invisibly return a \code{ggplot} object (\code{inherits(p, "ggplot")}).
Setting \code{draw = FALSE} constructs and returns the \code{ggplot} object without
printing it or opening a graphics device.

Update scripts that previously indexed \code{p$data$...} on a returned
\code{mfrm_plot_data} list to call \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} (\code{plot_data(p)} for the full
payload list, \code{plot_data(p, component = ...)} for a named table/matrix, or
\code{plot_data(p, "notes")} for descriptions and cautions) and
\code{\link[=plot_data_components]{plot_data_components()}} to inspect available components.

Saved plot payloads from \verb{0.2.x} (\code{mfrm_plot_data},
\code{mfrm_design_evaluation_plot_data}, and \code{mfrm_signal_detection_plot_data})
remain compatible with both \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} and \code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}}, and calling
\code{as_ggplot(p)} on a \verb{0.3.0} \code{ggplot} return value is idempotent.
Two-continuous-variable scatterplots (including the new
\code{plot(dif, type = "scatter")} route after \code{\link[=analyze_dff]{analyze_dff()}}, model comparisons,
fit pathway, fair average, bias, PCA scores, and recovery scatterplots) add
aligned X/Y marginal histograms via \code{marginal = c("auto", "histogram", "none")}.
These changes do not add support for previously unsupported models or
uncertainty calculations.
}

\section{Structured fit_mfrm() signature and shared spec}{

\code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} now exposes a focused 19-argument signature (\code{data}, 15 common
model/design arguments, \code{spec = NULL}, \code{control = list()}, and \code{...}) that
partitions all 55 canonical arguments into \code{data} (1), \code{spec} (33 model,
scale, identification, anchor, interaction, and population keys), and
\code{control} (21 quadrature, optimizer, shrinkage, JML correction, and runtime
keys).
\itemize{
\item \strong{Full-name flat arguments remain supported}: Supply the complete names
of the 55 canonical arguments directly to \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} or in the named list
passed to \code{do.call(fit_mfrm, ...)}. Their defaults and statistical meaning
are unchanged. Only the first four positional arguments (\code{data}, \code{person},
\code{facets}, \code{score}) retain their old positions; name all later arguments.
Abbreviations for arguments now routed through \code{...} are rejected. For
example, replace \code{quad_point = 15} with \code{quad_points = 15} or
\code{control = list(quad_points = 15)}.
\item \strong{Shared 4-entrypoint \code{spec}}: One named \code{spec = list(...)} can be passed
unchanged to \code{\link[=describe_mfrm_data]{describe_mfrm_data()}}, \code{\link[=review_mfrm_anchors]{review_mfrm_anchors()}}, \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}},
and \code{\link[=export_mfrm_model]{export_mfrm_model()}} (\verb{data = ..., spec = spec}) within each function's
supported scope. A valid native fit specification is not necessarily
exportable: ordinary RSM/PCM export requires a fixed standard-normal
population; estimated intercept-only normal populations are supported only
for one-family GPCM in Stan. Explicit top-level arguments override entries
in \code{spec}; misplaced or duplicated keys fail with actionable guidance.
\item \strong{Technical reference page}: Specialist equations, identification rules,
JML polish-ladder details, and return-object provenance now live in
\link{fit_mfrm_technical_details} (linked from \code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}}). Use
\code{mfrmr_output_guide("core_workflow")} for the five-step onboarding map.
}

